WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.684
Welcome to Grade 8 English, Unit 5:
Natural Resources and Sustainability.

2
00:00:04.764 --> 00:00:10.622
In this unit, you will explore the important
topic of natural resources and sustainability.

3
00:00:10.702 --> 00:00:18.258
You will learn how communities around the world—and especially
in East Africa—depend on natural resources like water,

4
00:00:18.338 --> 00:00:25.436
fish, forests, and clean air, and what happens when
those resources are threatened by pollution, carelessness,

5
00:00:25.516 --> 00:00:26.872
or outdated practices.

6
00:00:26.952 --> 00:00:32.354
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

7
00:00:32.434 --> 00:00:40.298
By the end of this unit, you will be able to understand and
use 31 new vocabulary words related to thought, strategy,

8
00:00:40.378 --> 00:00:44.847
environment, and persuasion in
your reading, writing, and speaking.

9
00:00:44.927 --> 00:00:50.958
You will also identify and construct complex
noun phrases using pre-modifiers (adjectives,

10
00:00:51.038 --> 00:00:57.340
determiners) and post-modifiers (prepositional
phrases, relative clauses, participle phrases).

11
00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:05.420
You will also form and use the future passive voice (will
be + past participle) to describe actions that will happen in

12
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.167
the future.

13
00:01:06.247 --> 00:01:08.679
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:08.759 --> 00:01:15.159
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
falter, pristine, deceptive, naive, antonym, tactic.

15
00:01:15.239 --> 00:01:20.515
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:20.595 --> 00:01:24.680
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:30.747
The reading sequence includes Natural Resources
Under Pressure and How Scientists Read a Coastline.

18
00:01:30.827 --> 00:01:31.605
Read actively.

19
00:01:31.685 --> 00:01:35.833
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

20
00:01:35.913 --> 00:01:39.816
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

21
00:01:39.896 --> 00:01:43.996
The first language focus is What Is a Complex Noun Phrase?

22
00:01:44.076 --> 00:01:48.825
A complex noun phrase is a group
of words built around a head noun,

23
00:01:48.905 --> 00:01:54.807
and the extra words give detailed information
about a person, place, thing or idea.

24
00:01:54.887 --> 00:01:59.564
Writers use complex noun phrases
to pack meaning into fewer words,

25
00:01:59.644 --> 00:02:04.393
which makes academic writing more
efficient and more sophisticated.

26
00:02:04.473 --> 00:02:12.250
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

27
00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.293
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1: Story Discussion.

28
00:02:16.373 --> 00:02:20.809
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

29
00:02:20.889 --> 00:02:24.246
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

30
00:02:24.326 --> 00:02:27.818
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

31
00:02:27.898 --> 00:02:29.540
You are ready to begin.

32
00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:36.953
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

33
00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:40.547
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

34
00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:49.008
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
